{"t":15098,"dir":"out","frame":{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Every evening for thirty-one years, Edwin Marsh climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Gull Point light, wiping his hands on a paraffin-stained rag. He trimmed the wick, polished the lens, and wound the clockwork that turned the great beacon until dawn.\n\nHis wife had called the lighthouse a jealous mistress. She had been right. When she died in the hard winter of '09, he kept climbing anyway, because the ships still came, and the fog still rolled in.\n\nThe relief boat came every three months with flour, oil, and letters. This spring the new boy, Tobias, gripped the railing as though the sea might pull him in.\n\n\"Doesn't it get lonely?\" Tobias asked, one April afternoon.\n\nEdwin considered this. Below, waves broke white against the rocks, as they had for ten thousand years.\n\n\"The light isn't for me,\" he said. \"It's for whoever's out there, trying to find their way home in the dark.\"\n\nTobias squinted at the horizon. \"Have you ever seen anyone? Someone who needed it?\"\n\nEdwin was quiet for a long time. Then he reached into his coat and pulled out a folded, water-stained letter, its edges soft as cloth.\n\n\"Once,\" he said. \"A fishing boat, forty years ago. Four men. I kept the light burning all night, and they came in at dawn, cold and grinning.\" He tucked the letter away. \"I was young then. I thought that was the whole story.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know the light doesn't need a reply to matter.\" He stood, knees popping, and nodded toward the tower. \"Come on. Sun's going. Help me with the wick.\"\n\nThat night, from the gallery rail, Tobias watched the beam sweep across the black water. Somewhere out there, he thought, someone was steering toward it."}},"session_id":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000022","api_message_id":"msg_probe_1"}}
